<BaseLayout title="The Doors Had Just Opened — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Right after the SIGGRAPH deadline, the first weeks after China reopened: Shanghai in winter, Sanya in the tropics, Shenzhen from 116 floors up.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · January–February 2023</p>
  <h1>The Doors Had Just Opened</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">After the SIGGRAPH deadline: Shanghai in winter, Sanya in the tropics, Shenzhen straight up.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>17 days · January 27 – February 12, 2023</strong><br />
    Los Angeles ✈ Hong Kong airport ✈ Shanghai ✈ Sanya, Hainan ✈ Shenzhen → (metro and taxi) Hong Kong airport ✈ Taipei ✈ Los Angeles<br />
    six flights · 
    three weeks after China dropped arrival quarantine · the tail of Chinese New Year, Year of the Rabbit ·
    one night in an airport · two sunsets over the sea · one observation deck on the 116th floor
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-route.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-route.jpg'} alt="Map of the trip: in from Los Angeles to Hong Kong airport overnight, a flight to Shanghai, flights south to Sanya on Hainan and back up to Shenzhen, metro and taxi to Hong Kong airport, and home via Taipei." /></a>
    <figcaption>The whole loop, minus the Pacific. Blue legs were flights; the short red one, Shenzhen to Hong Kong airport, was metro and taxi. Lines show the order of places, not the routes flown.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Every January, a good part of the computer graphics world spends the same week not sleeping. The SIGGRAPH paper
    deadline falls in late January, and for the people who submit to it, the weeks before are a blur of experiments,
    figures and rewritten paragraphs. Then the deadline passes, the upload page closes, and there is a strange, weightless
    feeling: nothing is due tomorrow. In 2023 that feeling arrived at a very particular moment. On January 8th, China
    had dropped the quarantine it had required of arriving travellers for almost three years. For the first time since
    2020, you could simply get on a plane and go.
  </p>
  <p>
    So we did. On the morning of January 27th a silver suitcase stood packed by the door in Los Angeles, and seventeen
    days later we were home again. It was a trip that was mostly about family: hers in Shanghai, mine wintering in
    Sanya, and friends in Shenzhen. Most of those days belong to them and stay off this page. What is here is the
    in-between: the mornings out, and three very different Chinas. Shanghai in late winter was grey and chilly
    and still full of New Year decorations. Sanya, on the tropical island of Hainan, had palm trees, warm sea and
    a sunset shared with what looked like half the island. Shenzhen, the city that did not exist forty-five years ago, we mostly
    saw from above. It was the tail end of Chinese New Year, which in 2023 fell on January 22nd and opened the Year of
    the Rabbit, so there were rabbits everywhere: lanterns, statues, inflatables, even a cartoon rabbit on a popsicle.
  </p>
  <p>
    As with the other trips on this page, everything here is reconstructed from the camera roll: when and where each
    photo was taken, lined up in order. Some days have dozens of pictures and some have none, and a day with no
    photos is not a day when nothing happened. Where the record is thin, I've kept the story short rather than
    invent the parts I don't have.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting there: a night in Hong Kong's airport</h2>
  <p>
    The route from Los Angeles to mainland China ran, as it often did in those months, through Hong Kong. Getting there took a
    long day in the air and a night that never quite became a night. By the evening of January 28th we were at <strong>Hong Kong International
    Airport</strong>, with an early-morning connection to Shanghai and nowhere in
    particular to be in between. If you have done an overnight in a big airport you know the shape of it. The shops
    pull down their shutters one by one, the announcements get rarer, the cleaning machines come out and hum up and
    down the empty concourses, and travellers stake out rows of seats and try every possible angle for sleep. None of
    them work. The airport never fully goes dark; it just dims, like a city at three in the morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before the shops closed there was one last bakery counter open, and a lemon tart in a little paper box, topped with
    a raspberry and a square of dark chocolate, became dinner. Hong Kong has a serious pastry culture, from the
    egg tarts of its cha chaan teng diners to the French-style patisseries in its malls, and even an airport tart here
    is better than it needs to be. By breakfast time the menu boards for Hong Kong-style breakfasts were lit again,
    and then we were on an early flight to Shanghai Pudong.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hkia-lemon-tart.jpg'} alt="A lemon tart with a raspberry and a square of chocolate in a paper box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dinner, Hong Kong airport, 9 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Act I: Shanghai in winter</h2>
  <p>
    Shanghai in late January is not a postcard city. The sky is a flat, pearly grey most days, the damp cold gets
    through any coat, and the plane trees along the old French Concession streets are bare. The camera roll from those days is sparse, but it
    records a few outings: one morning into the old city, one to the seaside, one
    to an ancient water town, and one to the Bund.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-shanghai.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-shanghai.jpg'} alt="Map of Shanghai outings: Yu Garden on January 31, Jinshan City Beach on February 1, Fengjing water town on February 2, and the Bund on February 3." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Shanghai, January 31 to February 3: four mornings out.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Yu Garden and the Year of the Rabbit</h3>
  <p>
    On January 31st we took the metro into the old city, to <strong>Yu Garden</strong>. The garden itself is a
    classical Ming-dynasty garden of rockeries, ponds and pavilions, begun in 1559 by an official named Pan Yunduan as
    a place for his parents to enjoy their old age. Around it has grown a whole district of shops and restaurants built
    in the same style: dark timber, white walls, and roofs whose eaves curl up at the corners like the toes of a
    slipper. It is touristy, and it knows it, and at New Year it does not care. Every year the bazaar hosts a lantern
    show for the Spring Festival, and every year it is mobbed.
  </p>
  <p>
    That morning, nine days into the Year of the Rabbit, the main square was dominated by a tableau of giant rabbits in
    silk robes and golden crowns, flanked by figures from Chinese folklore, all of it lit up and glittering even in
    daylight. The sky, for once, was a clean winter blue. The crowd in front was enormous and mostly masked. A
    month earlier China had been in the middle of its first great COVID wave after dropping its restrictions, and the
    habit of wearing a mask in a crowd had not gone anywhere.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yu-garden-rabbits.jpg'} alt="A crowd in front of a large New Year display of rabbit figures in robes and crowns, framed by traditional buildings with upturned eaves at Yu Garden." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Yu Garden, nine days into the Year of the Rabbit.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Away from the main square, the lanes of the bazaar were a canyon of carved wooden balconies, gilded signboards and
    red lanterns, with a canopy of colored paper umbrellas strung overhead. In one courtyard stood a tree with
    golden leaves, an artificial one, its branches hung with red tags, the kind on which people write a wish for
    the new year. A golden rabbit perched on a roof edge beside it. The sound of the place is the sound of any Chinese
    holiday crowd: hawkers, a loudspeaker somewhere, parents calling children, and under it all the shuffle of
    thousands of feet on stone.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yu-garden-lane.jpg'} alt="A crowded lane in the Yu Garden bazaar, lined with dark wooden buildings, with colored paper umbrellas hanging overhead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The lanes of the bazaar, under paper umbrellas.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'yu-garden-golden-tree.jpg'} alt="A golden ornamental tree in a courtyard of traditional buildings, with a crowd below and a golden rabbit on a roof." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A golden wishing tree, and a golden rabbit on the roof.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>A beach in winter</h3>
  <p>
    The next day we went to the seaside, which in Shanghai means <strong>Jinshan City Beach</strong> on Hangzhou Bay.
    The water off Shanghai's coast is famously brown, thick with silt carried down by the Yangtze, and Shanghai has no
    natural sandy beach to speak of. Jinshan's is man-made: imported sand, a promenade, palm trees, and a long
    breakwater that encloses a calmer swimming area. In summer it fills with families. On the first morning of
    February it was close to empty. The sea was the color of pewter, the sky a pale lid of cloud, and the giant blue
    letters of the "BEACH" sign stood on the dune with nobody to photograph them but us.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jinshan-beach-sign.jpg'} alt="Giant blue BEACH letters among bushes on a dune, with an empty sandy beach and a line of buoys under a grey sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Jinshan City Beach, February 1st.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'jinshan-beach.jpg'} alt="An empty promenade with palm trees and low buildings beside a wide, empty winter beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Palm trees, but not palm-tree weather.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is something likeable about a summer place in winter. The palm trees look slightly embarrassed, the
    promenade is swept and silent, and the only sounds are the wind and the flat slap of small waves. We did the
    obvious thing and bought an ice cream. It was a pink popsicle molded into a little picture of the beach itself,
    with the sun, a sailboat and the characters for "Jinshan City Beach" pressed into it, and it was eaten very
    quickly, which in that weather says something about how good it was.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'jinshan-popsicle.jpg'} alt="A pink popsicle molded with a beach scene and the characters for Jinshan City Beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The beach, in popsicle form.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On February 2nd we spent the late morning in <strong>Fengjing</strong>, one of the old water towns of the Yangtze
    delta, a few miles from the border with Zhejiang. The town is built along canals rather than streets, with stone
    bridges, whitewashed houses and black-tiled roofs reflected in the water. It is famous for its braised pork
    trotters and as the hometown of the cartoonist Ding Cong. The camera roll from that morning is almost all video and
    people, so there is nothing to show here, and I'll leave it at that.
  </p>

  <h3>The Bund</h3>
  <p>
    On February 3rd we went to see the view that is on every Shanghai postcard, and it is just as good grey as it is
    blue. From the promenade of the <strong>Bund</strong>, the riverfront lined with the granite banks and trading
    houses of the 1920s and 30s, you look across the Huangpu River at <strong>Lujiazui</strong>. Each tower over there
    is famous on its own. The pink-and-silver spheres of the Oriental Pearl Tower went up in 1994. The pagoda-like
    Jin Mao Tower followed in 1999, and the Shanghai World Financial Center, nicknamed "the bottle opener" for the
    trapezoidal hole in its top, in 2008. Then came the twisting, 632-metre <strong>Shanghai Tower</strong>, the tallest
    building in China, finished in 2015. The astonishing thing is the timing. In 1990, when the government declared
    Pudong a development zone, almost all of this was warehouses and farmland.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'bund-lujiazui.jpg'} alt="The Lujiazui skyline across the Huangpu River from the Bund promenade: the Oriental Pearl Tower, the Shanghai Tower and their neighbors under a grey sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Lujiazui from the Bund, a Friday morning in February.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On a weekday morning in winter the promenade had room to breathe: a few people strolling or taking photographs
    along the railing, the river traffic sliding past, and the wind coming off the water. A few minutes later we were
    eating a round, flaky pastry in a foil cup, stamped with red characters. That was the end of Shanghai. The next day we flew south, and by evening we were two thousand
    kilometres away, and it was summer.
  </p>

  <h2>Act II: Sanya, at the southern edge</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Sanya</strong> sits at the southern tip of Hainan, the big tropical island off China's south coast, at
    about eighteen degrees north. That's the same latitude as Jamaica. It is China's answer to Hawaii, and in the weeks
    around Chinese New Year it is where half of northern China seems to go to get warm. When the border reopened,
    Sanya was also one of the first places Chinese travellers flocked back to, and it showed. Every sight we went to was
    busy, and every beach at sunset was full.
  </p>
  <p>
    Part of the pull was family: my parents were spending the winter there, as a lot of Chinese retirees do.
    We arrived on the evening of February 4th. The first photo is from a height, looking out over the curve of
    Sanya Bay at night. The city's hotels and apartment towers made a long chain of gold and pink lights along the
    water, the sea below was black and still, and the low cloud over it all glowed orange with the reflected light of
    the city. Coming from Shanghai, the air was the first shock: warm and soft and damp, the kind of air that makes you
    take off your coat before you have decided to.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sanya-bay-night.jpg'} alt="Night view over Sanya Bay: a curve of city lights along the water under low, glowing cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sanya Bay, the night we arrived.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next evening, February 5th, was the <strong>Lantern Festival</strong>, the fifteenth day of the first lunar
    month and the traditional end of the New Year season. That evening we were out along the seafront greenway of
    Sanya Bay, around Haiyue Square, where the camera roll holds only video, so the lanterns will have to remain
    imaginary here.
  </p>

  <h3>Nanshan: the Guanyin in the sea</h3>
  <p>
    February 6th was the big day, and the one with the most photographs. It started at <strong>Nanshan</strong>, a
    Buddhist cultural park about forty kilometres west of the city, on a headland by the sea. We arrived a little after
    noon. You enter through an enormous ceremonial gate: a long run of white walls and red-lacquered pillars topped with
    tiered roofs, its panels decorated with elephants and lotus flowers. That day, under a sky rippled with high white
    cloud, a steady river of visitors was flowing up the steps beneath it, carrying sun umbrellas.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-0206.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-0206.jpg'} alt="Map of February 6: the Nanshan gate and Guanyin statue at noon, then east along the coast to Tianya Haijiao for the afternoon and sunset." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>February 6: Nanshan at noon, Tianya Haijiao at sunset.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-gate.jpg'} alt="The large white and red ceremonial gateway of the Nanshan park, with crowds and sun umbrellas on the steps, under a rippled sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The gate at Nanshan, 12:13 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The reason everyone comes is visible from the gate: a white statue standing in the sea at the end of a long
    avenue. This is the <strong>Guanyin of Nanshan</strong>, the bodhisattva of compassion, 108 metres tall. Unveiled in
    2005, it is one of the tallest statues in the world. It stands on its own artificial island just offshore, linked to
    the land by a long causeway. The statue has three faces looking in three directions, one towards the sea and two
    back towards the land, each holding something different: a sutra, a string of beads, a lotus. The idea is that her
    protection reaches everyone, whichever way they come from.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-approach.jpg'} alt="A long avenue with flower beds and visitors with umbrellas leading to the distant white Guanyin statue." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The approach, with flower beds in New Year red.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-guanyin.jpg'} alt="The white Guanyin statue with a golden halo, towering over a crowd on the steps below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Guanyin of Nanshan, 108 metres tall.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Up close, the statue is so big that it stops looking like a sculpture and starts looking like weather. The white
    figure is lit hard by the tropical sun against the blue, with a gold halo behind her head, and people mill
    around her base like ants at the foot of a lighthouse. The day was hot even in February, and the
    whole crowd moved at the slow, deliberate pace of people trying to stay in the shade of their umbrellas.
  </p>
  <p>
    Along the shore, the park has set up a series of giant white hands, each one making one of the mudras, the
    symbolic hand gestures of Buddhist art. One holds its palm forward with a golden Dharma wheel set into it, thumb
    and forefinger touching in a circle, the gesture of teaching. They are clearly designed for one particular photo,
    and everyone takes it: line up the camera so that the distant statue appears inside the circle of the fingers,
    or cradled in the open palm. We were no exception.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-hand-wheel.jpg'} alt="A giant white sculpted hand making the teaching gesture, with a golden Dharma wheel on its palm." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The teaching gesture, with a Dharma wheel.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-hand-frame.jpg'} alt="A giant white hand with the Guanyin statue framed inside the circle of its thumb and finger." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The statue, framed by the fingers.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The third hand lies open, palm up, and from the right spot the statue appears to stand in it. It is a trick of perspective, but a gentle one, and it works best with the sea showing between the fingers.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-hands.jpg'} alt="Two giant white hands in the foreground with the Guanyin statue appearing to rest on one palm." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>And resting in the palm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    From a terrace at the end of the park you can see the whole arrangement at once: the statue on its round
    island platform, and the causeway curving out to it across the water, packed with visitors and lined with red flags.
    It was one in the afternoon, the sun was nearly overhead, and the sea had that pale, milky blue that tropical water
    gets at midday. A vegetarian ice cream from a stand by the path seemed to be the done thing, so we did it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'nanshan-causeway.jpg'} alt="The Guanyin statue on its island platform in the sea, reached by a long causeway lined with red flags and visitors." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The causeway out to the statue.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Tianya Haijiao, the ends of the earth</h3>
  <p>
    By late afternoon we had moved east along the coast to <strong>Tianya Haijiao</strong>, which translates as "the
    edge of the sky, the corner of the sea", or more simply, the ends of the earth. For most of Chinese history this
    really was the end of the known world: a malarial island at the bottom of the empire, where emperors sent officials
    who had fallen out of favor. The most famous exile was the Song-dynasty poet Su Shi, banished to Hainan in his
    sixties in 1097. Today the phrase is used of lovers who will stay together "to the ends of the earth", and the
    beach is one of the most visited in China.
  </p>
  <p>
    The coast here is a scatter of huge, rounded granite boulders, grey-black and smoothed by the sea, some standing on
    the sand and some out in the water like the backs of sleeping animals. At a quarter to five, the light was already
    going low and warm. Palm trees leaned out over the sand, families were spread under them, and the sea glittered
    silver in the backlight.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-palms.jpg'} alt="A sandy beach shaded by leaning palm trees, with dark boulders in the sea and visitors on the sand in late-afternoon light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Tianya Haijiao, 4:47 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-boulders.jpg'} alt="Dark granite boulders in a silvery sea, backlit by the low sun." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The boulders, backlit.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The sunset that evening was the reason most of the people on the island seemed to be on this beach. The sun went
    down almost exactly into the sea, a hard orange disk with a glittering path running straight to the sand, and the
    whole beach turned to silhouettes. People waded into the shallows with their shoes in their hands, posed for
    photographs, held up phones, and called to each other over the sound of the waves. A small
    boat crossed the path of light. For half an hour everything on the beach was gold.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-sunset.jpg'} alt="The sun low over the sea at Tianya Haijiao, with a glittering path of light and silhouetted people on the beach." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>6:23 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-waders.jpg'} alt="Silhouetted people wading in the shallows and taking photos as the sun touches the horizon, with a small boat offshore." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Everyone in the water, shoes in hand.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The most famous of the boulders stands at the end of the beach. It is carved with two big red characters,
    <strong>天涯</strong>, "the edge of the sky". The inscription dates from the Qing dynasty, carved in the eighteenth
    century by a local official, and it gave the whole place its name. Up close the rock is huge, taller than a house,
    streaked with black and pale grey where rain has run down it for centuries, and the red characters are the size of
    a person.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-rock.jpg'} alt="A huge dark granite boulder carved with the red characters 天涯, the edge of the sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>天涯, "the edge of the sky".</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    As the sun touched the horizon, a crowd packed in around the boulders to watch it go down between them. The sun
    slid into a gap between two great rocks, a plane crossed the sky above, and a
    hundred phones went up at once. It is a strange experience, watching a sunset at the ends of the earth with that
    many people. It's not lonely and it's not quiet, but there is something nice about it all the same: a whole beach of
    strangers who have come a long way to look at the same thing.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-crowd.jpg'} alt="A crowd of silhouetted people on the sand photographing the sun setting between two giant granite boulders, with a plane overhead." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sunset between the boulders, 6:31 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then it was over, as fast as tropical sunsets always are. Five minutes after the sun went down, the sky had faded
    to a pale peach and grey, people were sitting on the rocks at the water's edge just looking out, and the ships at
    anchor in the bay had turned into small dark shapes.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-dusk.jpg'} alt="Visitors sitting and standing among boulders at the water's edge after sunset, under a fading sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>After the sun, 6:37 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'tianya-last-light.jpg'} alt="A calm grey sea at dusk with ships at anchor and a band of pink light on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Last light over the anchored ships.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Phoenix Island from above</h3>
  <p>
    Sanya Bay's most recognizable landmark is <strong>Phoenix Island</strong>, an artificial island built out in the
    bay and joined to the city by a causeway. One end holds a cruise terminal; the other holds a row of towers
    shaped like enormous eggs, ribbed with curving balconies and topped with open steel crowns. Seen from the
    shore, the row of them looks like something from a science-fiction film, and they show up in nearly every
    photograph of the city.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-sanya.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-sanya.jpg'} alt="Map of Sanya Bay: Haiyue Square on February 5, Phoenix Island on February 8, then Luhuitou hill and the dried-goods market downtown on February 9." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Sanya Bay, February 5 to 9.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    On the afternoon of February 8th we had afternoon tea at a bar high up, looking down on all of this. The tea came
    on a stand shaped like a birdcage, with tiny cakes in the brightest colors a pastry chef could manage, plus one
    bright green drink and one pink one. The sea filled every window behind it.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'sky-tea.jpg'} alt="An afternoon tea of small colorful cakes on a birdcage-shaped stand, with a green drink and a pink drink, by a window over the sea." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Afternoon tea, in a birdcage.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The view was the real reason to be there. Between the angled fins of the building's facade you could see straight
    across the water to the city and the green hills behind it. Turning the other way, the egg towers were right
    there, close enough to count the balconies, their steel crowns catching the light. Beyond them, a white cruise ship
    lay alongside the terminal. Far out on the open water, dozens of small boats sat at anchor, scattered like grains
    of rice across the bay, with a drilling rig on the horizon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'phoenix-frame.jpg'} alt="A view of Sanya Bay and the city framed between two angled metal fins of a building facade." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The bay, between the fins.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'phoenix-eggs.jpg'} alt="Two egg-shaped towers with ribbed balconies and steel crowns, with a cruise ship and the bay behind them." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The egg towers, up close.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    I find this kind of architecture hard to judge. On the one hand, a row of giant eggs on an artificial island is
    about as subtle as a fireworks display. On the other hand, in the late afternoon light, with the blue water all
    around, the green headlands and the white ships, it was undeniably beautiful, and it is impossible to mistake for
    anywhere else. That is more than most skylines manage.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'phoenix-cruise.jpg'} alt="A large white cruise ship at the terminal, a tall slender tower beyond, and an egg-shaped tower in the foreground, with the bay and hills behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A cruise ship at the terminal.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'phoenix-boats.jpg'} alt="A tall slender tower against the sea, with dozens of boats anchored on the water and a drilling rig on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Dozens of boats at anchor in the bay.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Luhuitou, the deer that turned its head</h3>
  <p>
    The next morning we climbed the other famous viewpoint: <strong>Luhuitou</strong>, the forested headland at the
    south end of Sanya Bay. The name means "the deer turns its head", and it comes from a legend of the Li people,
    Hainan's indigenous inhabitants. A young hunter chased a deer through the mountains for days until it was trapped
    at the edge of this cliff with nowhere left to run. As he drew his bow, the deer turned its head to look at him and
    became a beautiful young woman. They married and settled here, and the city, so the story goes, grew from their
    family.
  </p>
  <p>
    The path up starts with a rock garden of carved figures. Three tall stone sages stand on a cliff of artificial
    rock among palms and a small waterfall, carved with the characters for fortune and prosperity, and that morning
    a stream of visitors was passing at their feet. Then the road climbs through the trees to the top.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-carvings.jpg'} alt="Large carved stone figures of sages set into an artificial cliff with a small waterfall and tropical plants, with visitors walking below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The rock carvings at the foot of the hill, 11:18 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The hilltop park has the best views in Sanya. Looking west, the whole of Sanya Bay curves away into the haze:
    Phoenix Island with its five egg towers in a row and a white ferry moored in front, the long line of the city along
    the shore, and the mountains of the interior rising behind. The sky that morning was the kind of blue you only get
    by the sea, with a parade of fat white clouds marching across it, and bougainvillea in flower along the paths.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-phoenix.jpg'} alt="Phoenix Island seen side-on across the water from Luhuitou: five egg-shaped towers in a row, a white ferry, the city and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Phoenix Island from Luhuitou: all five eggs.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-bay.jpg'} alt="A wide view over Sanya Bay from the wooded hilltop, with Phoenix Island, the curve of the city and puffy white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sanya Bay from the hilltop.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    From different points around the summit you get different Sanyas. To the southeast, a quiet green coast curls
    around a small cove with a pier and a sandy beach, the sea beyond it a deep, clean blue. To the north, the view drops
    straight down into the marina: a forest of white masts and yachts packed into the harbor, with apartment towers
    lined up along the waterfront behind them like an audience. It's a reminder of what Sanya has become in thirty
    years. In the 1980s this was a small fishing town.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-coast.jpg'} alt="A green coastline curving around a cove with a pier and a sandy beach under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The coast to the southeast.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-marina.jpg'} alt="A marina packed with white yachts below the hill, with apartment towers along the waterfront and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The marina, packed solid.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The statue that gives the hill its name stands at the very top, at the head of a long staircase painted with a
    mural and the words "love forever". It is a big stone group: the deer, head turned back over its shoulder, with
    the hunter and the woman beside it. Seen from the foot of the stairs against the noon sky, it is mostly silhouette.
    The hill is said to be a popular place to propose.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'luhuitou-deer.jpg'} alt="A large stone statue of a deer turning its head, with human figures, at the top of a painted staircase against a blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The deer that turned its head, at the top of the hill.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>Morels and birthday cakes</h3>
  <p>
    In the afternoon we went into the old part of town for some shopping of a very Chinese kind: dried goods.
    Hainan is famous for them, and the shops are like libraries of food. There were glass-topped bins of morel
    mushrooms, wrinkled and honeycombed, in neat rows; other mushrooms sliced and dried into pale curls; dried seafood,
    tea, nuts and roots. Everything was sorted by grade and stacked to the ceiling under bright lights. The smell inside
    is earthy and salty and slightly sweet, and it stays in your clothes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next door, a cake shop had filled its window with the most elaborate novelty cakes I have ever seen. There was a
    birthday cake topped with a pile of gold ingots and a bottle of baijiu, a cake shaped like a
    cartoon pig, one crowned with roses and another with a motorcycle, and several decorated with a "2023" for the
    new year. Every single one was a little sculpture, and none of them looked like they wanted to be eaten.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sanya-morels.jpg'} alt="Glass-topped bins of dried morel mushrooms and sliced dried mushrooms in a shop." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Morels, by the bin.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'sanya-cakes.jpg'} alt="A bakery window full of elaborate novelty cakes, one topped with gold ingots, one shaped like a pig, one with 2023 on it." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cake window. One of them says 2023.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h2>Act III: Shenzhen, straight up</h2>
  <p>
    On February 10th we flew from Sanya to <strong>Shenzhen</strong>, an hour and a bit north across the South China
    Sea. If Sanya is China's beach, Shenzhen is its machine room. In 1980, when Deng Xiaoping made it the country's
    first Special Economic Zone, it was a small border town facing Hong Kong across a river. Today it has more than seventeen million, and it builds a large share of the world's phones, drones and
    circuit boards. It is a city with almost no "old" in it, and it doesn't seem to miss it. We were there mainly to
    see friends, and the photos below are from the hours in between.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'daymap-shenzhen.jpg'}><img src={P + 'daymap-shenzhen.jpg'} alt="Map of Shenzhen: Shenzhen Bay at sunset on February 10, the Futian CBD and Ping An Finance Centre on February 11, then SEG Plaza in Huaqiangbei, and the Convention Center on February 12." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>Shenzhen, February 10 to 12.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Sunset over Shenzhen Bay</h3>
  <p>
    We saw the city first from a high floor in Futian, the central district, at half past five in the afternoon. Below
    was the Shenzhen you don't see on postcards: a dense field of older six- and eight-story apartment blocks, the
    "urban villages" that were once actual villages and are now packed with housing, with new forty-story towers rising
    out of them and cranes on the skyline. Beyond them was the long, flat water of <strong>Shenzhen Bay</strong>, with
    the mangroves along its edge and the towers of the far shore in a haze. The sky was heavy with cloud.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shenzhen-dusk.jpg'} alt="A view from a high floor over dense mid-rise apartment blocks and new towers to the grey water of Shenzhen Bay under heavy cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Futian and Shenzhen Bay, 5:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then, at about six, the sun dropped into the gap between the cloud and the horizon, and the whole view caught fire.
    For ten minutes the sky over the bay burned orange. The sun sat like a coal on top of the distant skyline, and a
    column of molten light ran across the water and the mudflats toward us. The low tide had left the bay a mirror of
    shallow water and sandbars, and every channel in it glowed. The mangroves at the edge of the bay, part of a nature
    reserve where migrating birds spend the winter, turned to a black lace border along the bottom of the frame.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shenzhen-bay-sunset.jpg'} alt="An orange sunset over Shenzhen Bay seen above apartment towers, the sun just above a distant skyline." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>6:06 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shenzhen-bay-glow.jpg'} alt="The sun setting into a distant skyline across Shenzhen Bay, with a column of orange light across the water and mudflats." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A column of light across the bay.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    By ten past six it was nearly over. The sun had gone behind the far towers, leaving the bay a sheet of dull copper
    and the clouds above it a smoldering orange-grey. It was the second great sunset over water in five days, and
    they could hardly have been more different: one on a beach in a crowd of thousands, the other through a window,
    with a whole city spread out below in silence.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'shenzhen-bay-dusk.jpg'} alt="Shenzhen Bay after sunset: dull copper water and mudflats, a distant skyline silhouetted under smoldering orange cloud." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Shenzhen Bay, 6:11 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>The Futian CBD</h3>
  <p>
    The next morning was bright and clear. In February 2023 COVID testing booths were still part of the street
    furniture in Chinese cities, a last leftover of the previous three years. We took the metro to the
    <strong>Futian Central Business District</strong>. Futian's CBD was planned from scratch in the 1990s as the new
    center of a new city. It is laid out on a grand north-south axis of parks and civic buildings, and around it stands
    a forest of glass towers, most of them less than twenty years old.
  </p>
  <p>
    Above all of them rises the <strong>Ping An Finance Centre</strong>: 599 metres of stone-ribbed steel and glass
    with a stepped, faceted crown. Designed by the American firm Kohn Pedersen Fox and finished in 2017, it is the
    second-tallest building in China after the Shanghai Tower, which we had seen from the Bund a week earlier. It was
    planned with a spire that would have taken it past 660 metres, but the spire was dropped over concerns about air
    traffic. From the street you can't see the top without tipping your head all the way back. Standing at its foot and
    looking straight up, the surrounding towers converge into the sky like the walls of a canyon.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'futian-cbd.jpg'} alt="The Ping An Finance Centre rising above the glass towers of the Futian CBD, seen from a tree-lined street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ping An Finance Centre over the Futian CBD.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'futian-towers.jpg'} alt="A cluster of glass skyscrapers in Futian under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Futian's glass forest.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The street level of the CBD is surprisingly green: wide boulevards lined with banyan trees, plazas with palms, and
    the sound of traffic softened by all the leaves. It is also very clean and very new. Then we went up.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pingan-looking-up.jpg'} alt="Looking straight up between glass skyscrapers, which converge toward a blue sky with white clouds." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Looking straight up, 2:34 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>116 floors up</h3>
  <p>
    The observation deck of the Ping An Finance Centre is on the 116th floor, more than half a kilometre above the
    street. The elevator ride up is fast enough to make your ears pop. When the doors open, the city
    is suddenly below you as if you were looking down from a plane. The deck's windows are set at an angle between
    heavy dark steel mullions, so in many places you are looking down past your own feet.
  </p>
  <p>
    Straight below, the CBD's axis was laid out like an architect's model. The green spine of the central park ran
    north. In the middle sat the <strong>Civic Center</strong>, with its vast blue roof and a yellow hump in the center,
    designed to suggest a giant bird spreading its wings. Behind it, Lianhua Mountain park was a dark green patch among
    the towers, and beyond that more towers went on and on until they faded into the mountains along the northern
    horizon, half hidden by a long bank of cloud.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pafc-civic-center.jpg'} alt="View from the Ping An observation deck between two window mullions, down onto the Civic Center's blue and yellow roof and the parks and towers of Futian." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Civic Center's blue wings, from 116 floors up.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pafc-window.jpg'} alt="A tall glass skyscraper far below, seen through the angled windows of the observation deck, with clouds over the city." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The angled glass of the deck.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    To the south, the view ran out over the low ground along the bay toward Hong Kong. Shenzhen Bay shone in the
    haze, and across the Shenzhen River the hills of the New Territories rose behind it. The glass reflected the sun
    and the room behind us, so every photograph has a ghostly layer of the deck itself superimposed on the city. The
    effect is oddly appropriate: the city and its reflection, both of them new.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pafc-bay.jpg'} alt="A hazy view south over the city to Shenzhen Bay and the hills beyond, with reflections in the observation deck glass." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>South toward Shenzhen Bay and Hong Kong.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Looking west, the towers of the CBD stood shoulder to shoulder far below, their rooftop helipads and crowns
    tiny from up here. The long grey roofs of the Convention and Exhibition Center lay at their foot, and a river of
    apartment blocks ran away beyond them toward the horizon. Around three o'clock, the clouds that had been sitting on
    the mountains all afternoon began to slide down over the northern districts. For a few minutes the edge of the
    city disappeared into white, as if the map simply stopped there.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pafc-convention.jpg'} alt="Skyscrapers of the Futian CBD far below, with the long grey roof of the Convention Center and a sea of buildings stretching to cloud-covered mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Convention Center and the towers of the CBD.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'pafc-cbd.jpg'} alt="A tall skyscraper and its neighbors far below, with green parks and a low bank of cloud over the northern districts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cloud sliding over the northern districts, 3:01 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>

  <h3>Huaqiangbei</h3>
  <p>
    Late in the afternoon we went a couple of kilometres east to <strong>Huaqiangbei</strong>, the electronics
    district that has been called the hardware capital of the world. It is a grid of multi-story malls where every
    floor is divided into tiny stalls, each selling one kind of thing: connectors, LEDs, phone screens, chargers,
    drones, chips by the reel. If you need a part, someone here has a box of ten thousand of them. Hardware startups from
    around the world come here to build prototypes in days that would take months anywhere else.
  </p>
  <p>
    Its landmark is <strong>SEG Plaza</strong>, a tower of more than seventy floors finished in 2000, with the electronics market in its
    podium, its base wrapped in red video screens. In May 2021, on a calm day, the tower began to visibly wobble.
    Thousands of people fled the building, and a nervous city watched it on livestreams for days. Engineers eventually
    blamed wind-induced vibration of the mast on its roof, which was later removed. From the street on a sunny
    February afternoon, it looked perfectly steady.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'seg-plaza.jpg'} alt="The tall SEG Plaza tower in Huaqiangbei, with a glass podium and red video screens at its base." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>SEG Plaza, Huaqiangbei, 4:52 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h3>One last Cantonese brunch</h3>
  <p>
    Our last morning in China, February 12th, began with a walk past the Convention and Exhibition Center, where the
    Ping An tower stood over the palms like an exclamation mark. It was the same tower we had been looking down from the
    afternoon before, now looking down on us.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'pingan-street.jpg'} alt="The Ping An Finance Centre rising behind palm trees and the Convention Center, seen from a street." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Ping An tower from the Convention Center, February 12th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Then came the meal any trip to Guangdong has to end with: a Cantonese brunch of dim sum. Shenzhen sits in the
    Cantonese-speaking south, a few miles from Hong Kong, and morning tea is a serious institution here: pots of tea,
    a table filling up with small plates and bamboo steamers, and a long, slow late morning. Our table filled up fast.
    There was <em>cheung fun</em>, slippery rice-noodle rolls drizzled with sweet soy and sesame sauce, served on a
    little wooden stand. There was a cool, wobbly coconut cake in white layers over a band of yellow. And there were
    crispy red rice rolls, a newer Cantonese favorite: shrimp wrapped in a crunchy fried layer and then in a thin,
    bright red rice sheet, served with a dipping sauce.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'cheung-fun.jpg'} alt="Rice-noodle rolls drizzled with dark sweet sauce, with bok choy, on a dish on a small wooden stand." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Cheung fun.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'coconut-cake.jpg'} alt="Slices of layered white coconut cake with a yellow band, topped with purple flower petals." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Coconut layer cake.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The red rice rolls are all about contrast: a soft, slippery rice sheet on the outside, a crunchy fried layer inside it, and a whole shrimp at the core, best eaten quickly before the crunch goes soft.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'red-rice-roll.jpg'} alt="Crispy shrimp rolls wrapped in bright red rice sheets on a white plate with a dipping sauce." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Crispy red rice rolls.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>Home, the long way round</h2>
  <p>
    That afternoon we crossed back to Hong Kong the unglamorous way, by metro to the border and a taxi on the other
    side, and by early evening we were at <strong>Hong Kong International
    Airport</strong> again, two weeks after the long night there. The bakeries were open this time, and two slices of
    cake in a white box, a tiramisu and a chocolate-and-orange mousse cake, got us to the gate.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + 'hkia-cake.jpg'} alt="A slice of layered tiramisu cake in a white box." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hong Kong airport, February 12th, 5:48 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The way home went through <strong>Taipei</strong>, with a short connection at Taoyuan airport late that evening.
    Taoyuan has decorated its transit areas with small exhibitions about Taiwan, and the one by our gate, at half
    past ten at night, was an indoor garden of palms with a pair of carved canoes. They are in the style of the Tao people
    of Orchid Island off Taiwan's southeast coast, whose plank boats are painted white with red and black triangles and
    concentric eyes on the prow. A wall nearby introduced the sixteen officially recognized Indigenous peoples of
    Taiwan. It was a strange, gentle last stop: an empty terminal at night, a tropical garden under fluorescent light,
    and a long flight east ahead.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + 'taoyuan-garden.jpg'} alt="An indoor garden of palm trees in an airport terminal, with white canoes decorated with red and black patterns." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Taoyuan airport, transit, 10:32 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Thanks to the date line, a flight that leaves Taipei late at night lands in Los Angeles earlier the same day, so
    on paper a trip like this ends a few hours before it finishes. Seventeen days, three cities, two oceans' worth of
    sunsets, and a lot of rabbits. Looking back through the photos, what strikes me most is the range: a grey
    winter beach and a tropical one, a Ming garden and a 599-metre tower, a crowd of thousands at the ends of the earth
    and an empty airport at three in the morning. It was a good way to spend the weeks after a deadline. Nothing was
    due, and the doors had just opened.
  </p>
